      "out": "algebra-method",
      "description": "For a positive-length problem u²+bu=c with b and c positive, halve b; add the square (b/2)² to both equal areas; take the positive square root; subtract b/2. In modern notation: u=√(c+(b/2)²)−b/2. The repaired equality rules explain why the same area is added to both sides, and the filled first diagram explains why that area completes a square. First plot: √(40+9)−3=4, checked by 16+24=40. Second supplied plot: half of 8 is 4; 20+16=36; the positive outer side is √36=6; then y=6−4=2; check 2²+8×2=4+16=20. This reusable algebra also keeps quantities and units attached to an unknown; applying the same valid operation to both sides, including division by the same nonzero number, preserves an equality. The geometric argument here addresses positive lengths, not every signed or complex root.",
