      "description": "Keep each supplied date beside its observation and candidate prediction. The algebra method’s equal-quantity discipline gives residual e from predicted + e = observed: subtract the same predicted angle from both sides, so e = observed − predicted. No completing-square result is treated as an orbit.\nDate | observed | predicted | observed − predicted\n1 January 1605 | 30.0° ± 0.1° | 30.0° | 0.0°\n11 January 1605 | 40.2° ± 0.1° | 40.0° | +0.2°\n21 January 1605 | 50.8° ± 0.1° | 50.0° | +0.8°\nThe last two predictions lie outside the supplied ±0.1° observation intervals. This candidate fails these records at the stated tolerance. That does not by itself establish an ellipse, reject every circular model, identify a physical cause, or settle the arrangement of the cosmos. The differences are shown; no new calculation is required.",
